{"error":false,"activeTest":false,"task":{"id":14266458,"type":"Review","status":"Processed","importance":1,"reason":null,"locked":null,"proofId":null,"currentQuestionId":null,"currentQuestionTimestamp":null,"progress":1,"points":5,"passed":1,"rawPointsAwarded":7,"pointsAwarded":7,"mp":null,"mpAwarded":null,"mpDecayRate":null,"student":{"id":57975,"firstName":"Harrison","lastName":"Charles","locale":null,"subscriptionId":"sub_1UO3yHD4gcTFRx97dYjQXmX1","sequenceId":19},"course":{"id":111,"name":"Mathematical Foundations II"},"topic":{"id":1109,"name":"The Product Rule for Differentiation"},"test":{"id":null,"name":null,"diagnostic":null,"timeLimit":null,"questionCount":null},"created":"2026-10-16T12:48:30.000Z","started":"2026-10-16T12:56:21.000Z","completed":"2026-10-16T12:57:09.000Z","processed":"2026-10-16T12:57:22.000Z","progressStr":"100%","answers":[{"id":62377817,"created":"2026-10-16T12:56:36.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":14433,"number":1,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 12:56 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:14","question":{"id":47954,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":3635,"text":"<p>Given that $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}e^x,$ find $f'(x).$</p>\n","choice1":"$ \\dfrac{e^x+2xe^x}{2\\sqrt{x}}$","choice2":"$ 1+\\dfrac{e^x}{2\\sqrt{x}}$","choice3":"$\\dfrac{e^x+xe^x}{\\sqrt{x}}$","choice4":"$\\dfrac{1+2e^x}{2\\sqrt{x}}$","choice5":"$x+\\dfrac{2e^x}{\\sqrt{x}}$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = \\sqrt x$ and $v(x)=e^x.$ So, we apply the product rule:</p>\n<p>\\begin{align*}\nf'(x)\t&= (\\sqrt x)' \\cdot e^x + \\sqrt x \\cdot(e^x)'\\\\[3pt]\n \t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}e^x +\\sqrt{x}e^x\\\\[3pt]\n                                &= \\dfrac{e^x+2xe^x}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{align*}</p>\n","topic":{"id":1109,"name":"The Product Rule for Differentiation"},"example":{"id":3635,"title":"Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"},"group":{"id":94479,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[{"id":null,"number":null,"answers":[]}],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14266458,"answer":{"id":62377817,"cread":"2026-10-16T12:56:36.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":14433},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62377861,"created":"2026-10-16T12:56:51.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":10616,"number":2,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 12:56 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:11","question":{"id":48516,"format":"MC","difficulty":2,"exampleId":3636,"text":"<p>If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x \\sin{x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi.$</p>\n","choice1":"$-e^{\\pi}$","choice2":"$e^{\\pi}$","choice3":"$-\\pi e^{\\pi}$","choice4":"$\\pi e^{\\pi}$","choice5":"$0$","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin x.$ Then, we have</p>\n<p>\\[\n        \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=e^x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\cos{x}.\n\\]</p>\n<p>Applying the product rule gives</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} &= u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}+v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\\\[5pt]\n                                &=e^x \\cdot \\cos{x} + (\\sin{x}) \\cdot e^x\\\\[5pt]\n                                 &=e^x(\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}).\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>To find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi$ into the formula and get</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\t\t\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{x=\\pi}\n\t                  &=\\left.e^x(\\cos{x} + \\sin{x})\\right|_{x=\\pi}\\\\[5pt]\n\t                  &=e^{\\pi}(-1 + 0) \\\\[5pt]\n\t                 &=-e^{\\pi}.\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Therefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-e^{\\pi}.$</p>\n","topic":{"id":1109,"name":"The Product Rule for Differentiation"},"example":{"id":3636,"title":"Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"},"group":{"id":94505,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14266458,"answer":{"id":62377861,"cread":"2026-10-16T12:56:51.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":10616},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyModerate","difficultySymbol":"M","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62377905,"created":"2026-10-16T12:57:08.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":null,"freeResponse":"[\"3^x\\\\ln(3)\\\\cos(x)\"]","timeElapsed":12480,"number":3,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 12:57 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:12","question":{"id":311231,"format":"FR","difficulty":1,"exampleId":3635,"text":"Given that $f(x) = 3^x \\cos(x) ,$ find $f'(x).$","choice1":"[math]3^x\\left( \\ln 3  \\cos x-\\sin x \\right)[/math]","choice2":"[math]3^x\\left( \\ln 3  \\cos x+\\sin x \\right)[/math]","choice3":"[math]3^x\\left( \\cos x-\\sin x \\right)[/math]","choice4":"[math]3^x\\left( \\ln 3  \\sin x+\\cos x \\right)[/math]","choice5":"[math]3^x\\left( -\\ln 3  \\cos x-\\sin x \\right)[/math]","freeAnswerChoice":" \\newline\n$f'(x)=$\n[\n    answer: 3^x \\ln(3) \\cos(x);\n    type: expression;\n    width: 120;\n    margin-left: 2;\n    margin-right: 0;\n    buttons: e, ln, sin, cos, exp;\n] $ -\\, 3^x \\sin(x)$","explanation":"<p>Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) =3^x $ and $v(x)=\\cos(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:</p>\n<p>\\[\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x)\t\n\t\t&=  \\left(3^x\\right)' \\cos(x)+ 3^x \\left(\\cos(x) \\right)' \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\left(3^x \\ln(3)\\right) \\cos(x)+ 3^x\\left( -\\sin(x)\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=  \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{3^x \\ln(3) \\cos(x)} - 3^x \\sin(x)\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\\]</p>\n","topic":{"id":1109,"name":"The Product Rule for Differentiation"},